ernmental and  public bodies and other relevant sources  I shall deal with each intern gathering  witness statements from individuals who  are infected or affected is a priority  for the inquiries work for two reasons  firstly the poor and deteriorating  health of a number of them means we want  to receive as many witness statements  early in the inquiry as we can  secondly as the chair has explained the  experiences of infected and affected  people the accounts they have to give  lie at the heart of this inquiry on the  2nd of July immediately upon being set  up the inquiry invited people who were  infected or affected in consequence of  infected blood products or blood to  complete a short form telling the  inquiry whether and if so how they would  like to give evidence we received over  1,300 such forms and since then many  further individuals have come forward  and we expect currently in the region of  about 2,200 odd statements from infected  or affected people but over the coming  months the inquiry has begun the process  of obtaining witness statements so far  in terms of the enquiry zone work is  concerned it's identified around 650  individuals from whom the inquiry team  itself will be taking statements you do  not need legal representation in order  to provide evidence to the inquiry and  individuals who feel able to can draft  their own statements or really enquiry  will assist with the preparation of the  statement for everyone who wants to help  but for the many individuals who are  legally represented we will be asking  there  legal representatives to obtain those  statements and we will be finding the  costs of so doing this is a process that  will continue over the coming months the  focus of these statements will be the  individual personal experiences of those  who were infected and the individual  personal experiences of their families  and loved ones we understand that it  will not be an easy task but a number of  those who will be giving statem